    21	same 200 evaluation seeds, same frozen reference per rule (checked: `seeds` and reference `upstream` hashes equal):
    22	
    23	| Rule | g(D2+H3) | g(D2) | I = g(D2+H3) - g(D2) | Paired SE of I | D2+H3 doubling check |
    24	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    25	| B2 | 0.5851 | 0.0027 | 0.5824 | 0.0011 | not scheduled |
    26	| B3 (F000) | 0.3502 | 0.0435 | 0.3067 | 0.0012 | flagged |
    27	| R3 (F111) | 0.0486 | 0.0361 | 0.0124 | 0.0009 | flagged |
    28	| C* | 0.0872 | 0.0433 | 0.0439 | 0.0008 | not flagged |
    29	
    30	Source: `results/v2/eval/all/evaluate/headline@rho0.2__{rule}__{D2+H3,D2}__profit.json`, field `gain`; flags from
    31	`report.json` `search_flagged`; the D2 cells had no scheduled doubling check. At `headline@rho1`, R3 and C* are in the
    32	terminal-reserve collapse and I is 0.0025.
    33	
    34	Reading, exploratory: at the headline market the D2+H3 search leaves about 0.31 more gain than the D2 search against B3,
    35	and about 0.01 against R3; the B3 - R3 gap is 0.302 under D2+H3 and 0.007 under D2.
    36	
    37	## 2. Estimand
    38	
    39	For rule r in {B3 (F000), R3 (F111), C*} and fresh market m:
    40	
    41	I(r, m) = mean over the 200 evaluation seeds of g(D2+H3 winner) - g(D2 winner),
    42	
    43	both against the same frozen truthful reference of (r, m), so the reference term cancels. **I is a difference between two
    44	prescribed finite searches over nested families (D2 within D2+H3), not an identified optimal value of replacement and not
    45	a system-wide prohibition of replacement.** The two searches differ in dimension, Sobol starts, and corner sets (D2 128,
    46	D2+H3 256 corner vectors), with the same restart and evaluation budgets; the two winners can differ in every knob, not
    47	only `x`. A negative I can come from search error. The fixed-policy diagnostic in section 4 separates the switch from
    48	re-optimisation.
    49	
    50	## 3. Cells and execution contract
    51	
    52	- **New search cells.** D2 x {F000, F111, C*} x fresh0..fresh5 at rho 0.2: 18 cells, exactly the design-8.1 cells that cut
    53	  3 removed, with their registered settings (D2 knob space and budget, the market's training seeds for search, its 200
    54	  evaluation seeds), each with a doubling check (design 6.5, held-out evaluation of the doubled winner on the same seeds).
    55	- **Driver.** A separate follow-up script (`docs/revision/followup/reset_followup_v2.py`, under `docs/` because the code
    56	  freeze allows changes only there; written and tested before any spend) that
    57	  imports the frozen stage functions of `scripts/revision_v2.py` at the protocol-v2 tag (`job`, `stage_search`, the
    58	  evaluation and doubling stages) and builds the 18 jobs from the uncut cell list. It bypasses only three things, each
    59	  recorded as a deviation: the fixed manifest path and cut list (`sim/manifest_v2.py` `effective`), the hard-coded doubling
    60	  eligibility (`doublings`), and the original freeze. It writes to a new directory (`results/v2/followup-reset/`) and
    61	  never writes to `results/v2/eval/all`.
    62	- **Inputs the frozen stages read.** The search, evaluation, and doubling stages read their dependencies from the output
    63	  directory (`menu()`, `reference_of()`, `select_reference()`). Before any episode, the driver copies into the new
    64	  directory, read-only, every record those stages read for the 18 cells: the frozen reference records, the search
    65	  records and bank-source records that build each (context, rule) menu, and the original cuts-3 bank membership. It
    66	  takes the list from the `upstream` fields of the existing records and checks each copy's SHA-256 against them; it
    67	  refuses on any missing record or mismatch.
    68	- **Two freezes.** A pre-evaluation freeze after the 18 searches: selected policies, references, configurations, seeds,
    69	  and search records, with their hashes (the frozen evaluation stage requires it). Evaluation and doubling then run
    70	  against that freeze, and a final inventory lists every output record with its hash. The inventory is written after
    71	  the outputs, so no record hashes itself.
    72	- **Freeze validator.** The frozen `run()` calls `accept()`, which requires the original manifest's full record set, so it
    73	  cannot admit the 18 restored cells. The driver therefore has its own acceptance check, run before evaluation and before
    74	  doubling: every record of the follow-up dependency set (the copied inputs, the 18 search records, the pre-evaluation
    75	  freeze) exists and matches its hash; the identity-salt hash matches; each new job's configuration hash equals the
    76	  recorded container hash. Copied records keep their original provenance, including their recorded configuration
    77	  hashes. Tests: it refuses a missing freeze, a changed selected-policy record, a changed salt, and a changed copied input.
    78	- **References in doubling.** The held-out gain of a doubled winner uses the unchanged frozen reference. Any reference
    79	  reselection the doubling stage computes is recorded as a diagnostic only.
    80	- **References.** The existing frozen reference records are read, not re-selected; the driver refuses to start if any
    81	  reference file's SHA-256 differs from the hash in the original records' `upstream`.
    82	- **Configurations.** Linux pods from the same image as the evaluation; before any episode the driver checks that each
    83	  fresh market's configuration hash equals the recorded container hash (`addendum.json` `deviations.configs`), so the
    84	  new cells use the same configurations as the D2+H3 records they are paired with (the recorded 1-ulp deviation stays as
    85	  it is).
    86	- **Pairing and code checks (draft 5).** Before any episode the driver refuses a job whose runner, salt, configuration,
    87	  root, seeds, rule parameters, or context differ from its frozen reference's provenance; an unknown commit; a runner or
    88	  `sim/` that differs from protocol-v2; an output inside `--src` or `eval/all`; and a salt mismatch. The inventory records
    89	  Python, numpy, machine, platform, and the driver's hash.
    90	- **Validation before spend (free, on the Mac).** (a) Reproduction of the registered `headline@rho0.2|F111|D2` search and
    91	  evaluation. Draft 4 required identical knobs and per-seed gains. Measured on 2026-11-09/10: a Mac arm64 run and a Linux
    92	  amd64 container run both match on configuration, salt, runner, budget, rule parameters, episode count (15,660), archive
    93	  sizes, and the twin result, with selected knobs equal to 2.6e-13 relative and per-seed gains to 2.0e-14; evaluating the
    94	  registered knobs on the Mac gives per-seed gains within 5e-17 of the record (102 of 200 seeds bit-identical). The
    95	  evaluation of fixed knobs is not always this close: one of the 128 corner controls of the Mac rerun differs by 1.5e-5
    96	  with identical knobs (the other 127 within 2e-18), consistent with a near-tie flipping a discrete branch in an episode;
    97	  in the Linux container all 128 controls are bit-identical to the record. The drift source is therefore not isolated to
    98	  the optimiser. Last-bit
    99	  arithmetic differences grow through the search; these runs did not reproduce bit identity across platforms, and the
   100	  draft-4 rule is replaced as a recorded deviation. **Draft-5 criterion** (`compare()`). Exact: the provenance keys runner, salt, configuration, rule
   101	  parameters, root, training, validation and evaluation seeds, context and budget (the commit may differ); the episode
   102	  count; the attack and twin archive sizes; `best_source`; the discrete knobs; the search and evaluation dependency ids;
   103	  the reference hash; the control set (knobs in order). Within 1e-9: the twin's best value. Reported, not gated: the
   104	  corner controls' objective values (count and largest difference), because a near-tie in a non-selected corner policy
   105	  can flip a discrete branch. Finite: the knobs, the attack, twin and control objective values, and the per-seed gains. Tolerance: continuous knobs within 1e-9 relative and per-seed
   106	  gains within 1e-9 absolute (about 10^4 times the drift measured, and far below the paired standard error of about
   107	  0.0009). The Mac runs pass this criterion. **Pod gate:** the pod first reruns the same reproduction (about 0.34 USD of
   108	  billed time: 2,084 s search plus evaluation at 0.56 USD per hour); the 18 cells start only if it passes the draft-5
   109	  criterion; a bit-identical result on the pod is reported as such. The reproduction path runs every pre-episode check
   110	  (salt, commit, code, pairing, configuration) before its search and saves `gate.json` and an inventory with the
   111	  environment (Python, numpy with BLAS and LAPACK, SciPy, CMA, CPU). (b) An
   112	  integration test runs the complete path (input copies, search, pre-evaluation freeze, evaluation, doubling, inventory)
   113	  for one fresh-market cell at smoke size, including the driver's acceptance check, which proves every dependency
   114	  resolves (passed, 2026-11-09). (d) Doubling ground truth: the driver's doubling stage reruns the registered doubling
   115	  check of `fresh0@rho0.2|F111|D2+H3|profit` on the Mac (`--reproduce-doubling`), after the full preflight, saving a record with provenance and dependency
   116	  hashes, `gate.json`, and an inventory; compared by `compare_doubling()`: required fields present; finite values; exact on
   117	  the flag, the seeds and the discrete knobs; 1e-9 on the continuous held-out knobs, the attack and reference
   118	  changes, and the held-out per-seed gains. The saved result must pass before any pod is created. (c) Unit tests: the driver refuses a
   119	  changed reference hash, a missing input record, a wrong configuration hash, and an output directory inside `eval/all`.
   120	
   121	## 4. Diagnostics (evaluation only, no search)
   122	
   123	For three rules on seven markets: the base and doubled D2+H3 winners (42 policies) and the base D2 winners (21; the doubled
   124	D2 winners are not included), on the 200 evaluation seeds, re-run with reset and ban instrumentation (63 single-policy
   125	evaluations; the existing gain records carry no reset counts, so none is reused); the selected `x`; per episode the number of resets, the probability of at least one reset, the rounds of resets,
   126	and the rounds spent banned. Check that every D2 winner has zero resets. **Reverse switch (draft 5):** each of the 18 D2 winners re-evaluated with
   127	`x = 1` and every other knob unchanged (18 more evaluations). The diagnostics are built and tested in the driver (`diagnostics`
   128	stage) before any pod. Order: the instrumentation is validated on the registered headline cells before the pod; the
   129	diagnostics of the 18 new D2 winners and their reverse switches run on the Mac after the pod's records are retrieved.
   130	Construction: a D2 winner is embedded in D2+H3 by adding `x` (0 for itself, 1 for the reverse switch); each switch is
   131	the mean difference of normalised profits on the evaluation seeds, the reference cancelling. Resets are the focal
   132	provider's identities of generation 1 or more; ban rounds are the ban lengths imposed on its identities; rounds without
   133	an offer are counted separately. Saved per policy: the seed identifiers, the per-seed normalised profits, the per-seed switch differences, and the
   134	reset and ban counts per episode; a D2 winner with any reset stops the run. Spot check: `fresh0@rho0.2|F111` is also
   135	diagnosed on the pod (`--diag-cells`); the Mac's reset and ban counts must equal the pod's exactly, and its numerical
   136	values must
   137	meet the draft-5 tolerance against it. **Fixed-policy switch:** each of the 42 D2+H3
   138	winners re-evaluated with `x` set to 0 and every other knob unchanged (42 more; a winner that already has `x = 0` is
   139	reported as identical and not re-run); g(winner) - g(winner, x = 0) is the effect of the switch
   140	without re-optimisation, reported beside I.
   141	
   142	## 5. Analysis (fixed now)
   143	
   144	- **Intervals.** Per market: paired seed bootstrap (9,999 resamples, percentile, 95%), the same resampled seed indices for
   145	  every rule and family of that market, so contrasts across rules stay paired. Pooled: equal market weights; in each
   146	  replicate, seeds are resampled independently within each market and the pooled statistic is the mean of the six market
   147	  means; percentile interval. Nominal and descriptive; conditional on the six markets and the frozen policies and
   148	  references.
   149	- **Statistics.** I(r, m) and pooled I(r); the contrasts I(B3) - I(R3) and I(C*) - I(R3); the absolute gaps
   150	  G(D2+H3) = g_B3 - g_R3 under D2+H3 and G(D2) under D2, pooled.
   151	- **Share.** S = 1 - pooled G(D2) / pooled G(D2+H3), a ratio of pooled contrasts, computed in every bootstrap replicate.
   152	  S is reported only if the lower bound of pooled G(D2+H3) exceeds 0.05; otherwise "not defined". No clipping; S can be
   153	  negative or above 1. The absolute gaps are always reported beside it.
   154	- **Market sensitivity (draft 5).** The sign count of I(B3) - I(R3) and I(C*) - I(R3) over the six markets, and S with
   155	  each market left out in turn (six values), each with its own five-market denominator gate (lower bound of the
   156	  five-market pooled G(D2+H3) above 0.05; otherwise that value is "not defined"), for the base and the doubled results
   157	  separately. A nested best-of sensitivity (the better of the D2 and D2+H3 winners per cell) is deferred: it is not
   158	  part of this plan.
   159	- **Doubling flags.** For each D2 cell, the attack change and the reference-reselection change of its doubling check are
   160	  reported separately.
   161	- **Doubled versions** of the same statistics, as in the paper's `tab:ph-doubled`. The base (frozen) searches are primary,
   162	  as in the paper; the doubled versions are reported beside them.
   163	- No confirmatory test, no multiplicity family, no change to any registered decision.
   164	
   165	## 6. Pre-specified expectations and labels
   166	
   167	Written before any fresh D2 result exists. **The six markets, their seeds, and their D2+H3 outcomes were already observed;
   168	only the D2 outcomes are unseen.** This is a descriptive follow-up specified on 2026-11-09 after the round-9 review and
   169	after the headline contrast was seen, not an independent replication.
   170	
   171	Expectations: on all six fresh markets (each market counted; a single market against it fails the expectation), I(B3) > I(R3) and I(C*) > I(R3); pooled S defined and greater than 0.5. Expectations are
   172	scored on point estimates (this is a descriptive follow-up; "met" does not mean that an interval excludes the threshold),
   173	with the denominator gate of section 5 applied separately to the base and the doubled results. Each expectation is
   174	scored as met, failed, or not established: **met** if it holds under both the base and the doubled searches; **failed** if
   175	it fails under both; **not established (search-sensitive)** if the two disagree. An undefined S and a missing cell are
   176	reported as such, separately from failures. A failure is not read as evidence for any particular cause (search error and
   177	mixed outcomes are possible). Only if the pooled-S expectation is met may the paper say: within the D2 family, at the
   178	fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 - R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the
   179	attacker cannot replace its identity.
   180	
   181	## 7. Cost
   182	
   183	**Work.** Measured benchmark (`experiments/v2/benchmark/timings.json`): one unit = a headline D2+H3 cell, 0.579
   184	core-hours. Per D2 cell: 1 unit + 160 further seeds (0.046) + doubling with held-out evaluation (0.479); 18 cells = 27.45
   185	units. Diagnostics: 63 instrumented evaluations + up to 42 switch evaluations = up to 105 single-policy evaluations of

